item.text:
I searched official Mexican government, ASF, CONEVAL, OECD, and World Bank materials. The strongest additions concern Ramírez de la O’s qualifications and assigned portfolio, ENIGH-based distributional incidence, and the limited reach of minimum-wage gains among the extremely poor and informal workers. The World Bank homepage problem is resolved with a precise “Mexico Overview” locator, although the source remains retrospective.

The main unresolved gap is adjudication. ASF’s 2022 audit index and search-accessible extracts identified relevant Tren Maya and Dos Bocas audits, including contract observations, but repeated attempts to open the underlying audit PDFs and consolidated recovery matrix timed out. I therefore did not convert those extracts into evidence records or treat proposed recoveries as final loss, misconduct, or resolved liability. Searches also did not recover reliable event-level proof that AMLO granted Ramírez de la O professional autonomy beyond an assigned portfolio, or dated 2022 decisions responding to procurement, health-access, Pemex, or project-appraisal warnings.

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON:{"title":"Comunicado No. 044: Rogelio Ramírez de la O asume cargo como secretario de Hacienda y Crédito Público","publisher":"Secretaría de Hacienda y Crédito Público, Government of Mexico","publication_date":"2021-07-16","url":"https://www.gob.mx/shcp/prensa/comunicado-no-044-rogelio-ramirez-de-la-o-asume-cargo-como-secretario-de-hacienda-y-credito-publico?idiom=es-MX","claim":"Ramírez de la O entered office with a Cambridge economics doctorate, a UNAM economics degree, research on economic policy, debt, trade and monetary policy, and extensive private-sector advisory and independent-director experience. The presidency assigned Hacienda greater involvement in energy-sector accounts, development banking, the 2022 economic package and a multiyear financial plan. This establishes strong qualifications and a substantive delegated portfolio, but does not independently establish freedom to overrule AMLO or professional autonomy in contested decisions.","locator":"Paragraphs 53-71, especially 58-68","provisional_id":"WEB-5B-019","canonical_fact_key":"amlo_2022_finance_minister_qualifications_and_assigned_portfolio","disposition":"context","chapter_ids":["5B"],"methodology_ids":["5B.2"],"source_type":"official_government_appointment_statement","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary official statement precisely identifies credentials and assigned responsibilities, but it is self-descriptive and supplies no evidence of independence from the president in actual 2022 disputes.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Appointment predates 2022 but defines the minister and portfolio in force during 2022.","ruler_attribution":"AMLO appointed Ramírez de la O and the official statement expressly links the expanded Hacienda portfolio to the president's policy commitments; actual discretionary autonomy remains unproven.","contrary_evidence":"The statement emphasizes continuity with AMLO's transformation, austerity and fiscal commitments, which cautions against inferring independent authority merely from technical qualifications.","underlying_fact_key":"amlo_2022_finance_minister_qualifications_and_assigned_portfolio","lenses":["5B.2"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON:{"title":"The World Bank in Mexico: Mexico Overview","publisher":"World Bank","publication_date":"2025-10-07","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/country/mexico/overview","claim":"The World Bank states that Mexico's official multidimensional-poverty rate fell from 43.2% in 2016 to 36.3% in 2022, lifting 5.4 million people out of poverty, and attributes the change mainly to labor-market outcomes, including minimum-wage increases. This upgrades WEB-5B-015 with a precise underlying locator, but the wording does not isolate the minimum wage from reopening, labor demand, transfers or remittances.","locator":"Mexico Overview, Context section, lines 29-34, especially paragraph beginning 'Yet, the official multidimensional poverty rate'","provisional_id":"WEB-5B-020","canonical_fact_key":"mexico_2022_poverty_decline_world_bank_labor_market_attribution","disposition":"context","chapter_ids":["5B"],"methodology_ids":["5B.8"],"source_type":"international_organization_country_overview","source_confidence":"medium","source_confidence_reason":"Authoritative and precisely opened, but published retrospectively and provides a broad attribution rather than a documented causal decomposition.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Later source specifically reporting the 2016-2022 poverty change and a 2022 endpoint.","ruler_attribution":"Minimum-wage policy was an administration choice, but the national poverty outcome is shared and contextual; this source does not estimate AMLO's marginal causal contribution.","contrary_evidence":"The source does not separate policy effects from post-pandemic reopening, employment growth, remittances, transfers or other labor-market changes.","underlying_fact_key":"mexico_2022_poverty_decline_world_bank_labor_market_attribution","lenses":["5B.8"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON:{"title":"Informe de la pobreza multidimensional en México, 2022","publisher":"CONEVAL","publication_date":"2025-01","url":"https://www.coneval.org.mx/InformesPublicaciones/Documents/Pobreza_Multidimensional_2022.pdf","claim":"ENIGH estimates show that 13.6 million households received income from at least one social program in 2022, 3.2 million more than in 2018. Nevertheless, program income fell from 34.4% to 27.3% of total current per-capita income for recipient households in the first decile, rose from 4.9% to 5.4% in the tenth, and gained relative weight in middle and upper-middle deciles. Average program-income growth from 2018 to 2022 was only 3.5% in the first decile but 97.0% in the eighth.","locator":"PDF pp. 147-149, Graphs 20-22 and accompanying text; extracted lines 5871-5945","provisional_id":"WEB-5B-021","canonical_fact_key":"enigh_2022_social_program_incidence_by_income_decile","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["5B"],"methodology_ids":["5B.8"],"source_type":"official_household_survey_incidence_analysis","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official CONEVAL analysis based directly on ENIGH microdata with explicit decile definitions, program keys, tables and stable page locators.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Retrospective publication analyzing specifically measured 2022 household income and program receipt.","ruler_attribution":"The administration owned the federal transfer architecture, but incidence reflects program rules, household composition and survey reporting; national income changes should not be assigned solely to AMLO.","contrary_evidence":"Coverage expanded in aggregate and program income remained proportionally much more important to the first decile than the tenth, even though targeting shifted away from the poorest relative to 2018.","underlying_fact_key":"enigh_2022_social_program_incidence_by_income_decile","lenses":["5B.8"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON:{"title":"Informe de la pobreza multidimensional en México, 2022","publisher":"CONEVAL","publication_date":"2025-01","url":"https://www.coneval.org.mx/InformesPublicaciones/Documents/Pobreza_Multidimensional_2022.pdf","claim":"CONEVAL's accounting counterfactual estimates that, without reported social-program transfers, Mexico's 2022 multidimensional-poverty rate would have been 39.0% rather than 36.3% and extreme poverty 8.9% rather than 7.1%; the corresponding population differences were 3.5 million and 2.4 million. Including program income reduced the 2022 Gini from 0.449 to 0.431. These are static income-removal estimates, not causal estimates accounting for eligibility, behavior or general-equilibrium effects.","locator":"PDF pp. 154-156, Graphs 28-29 and inequality discussion; extracted lines 6160-6232","provisional_id":"WEB-5B-022","canonical_fact_key":"enigh_2022_social_transfer_accounting_counterfactual_poverty_inequality","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["5B"],"methodology_ids":["5B.8"],"source_type":"official_household_survey_counterfactual_analysis","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official ENIGH-based calculations with precise numerical locators and a transparent remove-transfer comparison; confidence concerns interpretation as causal, not the reported arithmetic.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Later analysis specifically estimates the incidence of transfers recorded in calendar-year 2022 ENIGH data.","ruler_attribution":"Federal programs were a central administration policy and their recorded transfers contributed mechanically to household income; the counterfactual does not identify AMLO's causal effect or separate program design from implementation and take-up.","contrary_evidence":"The favorable aggregate poverty effect coexisted with weaker relative incidence in the poorest decile and substantial noncoverage among people in extreme poverty.","underlying_fact_key":"enigh_2022_social_transfer_accounting_counterfactual_poverty_inequality","lenses":["5B.8"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON:{"title":"Análisis de la población en situación de pobreza extrema","publisher":"CONEVAL","publication_date":"2024-08","url":"https://www.coneval.org.mx/Medicion/MP/Documents/contribucion_estrategias_pobreza/Pobreza_extrema_Mexico.pdf","claim":"For people in extreme poverty in 2022, subordinate-work remuneration supplied 47.6% of per-capita current income, but practically all employed people in this group worked informally, so the statutory minimum-wage increase did not directly determine their income. Transfers supplied 22.2% of income and social programs accounted for 58% of those transfers, yet 4.1 million people in extreme poverty—45.1% of that population—reported that their household received no monetary social program.","locator":"PDF pp. 109-113, especially 'Principales hallazgos' on p. 111; extracted lines 2876-2917","provisional_id":"WEB-5B-023","canonical_fact_key":"mexico_2022_extreme_poverty_income_informality_transfer_coverage","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["5B"],"methodology_ids":["5B.8"],"source_type":"official_household_survey_group_incidence_analysis","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official CONEVAL analysis of ENIGH 2022 with explicit income shares, informality limitation and uncovered population counts.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Later publication specifically establishing conditions measured in 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Minimum-wage increases and federal transfers were administration policies, but this evidence sharply limits attribution because extreme-poverty workers were overwhelmingly informal and nearly half of the group reported no monetary-program receipt.","contrary_evidence":"Transfers were still a major income component and recipients had lower food and health deprivation than otherwise comparable descriptive groups, so the evidence is not wholly adverse.","underlying_fact_key":"mexico_2022_extreme_poverty_income_informality_transfer_coverage","lenses":["5B.8"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON:{"title":"Sistema de Indicadores sobre pobreza y género, 2016-2022","publisher":"CONEVAL","publication_date":"2024-03-06","url":"https://www.coneval.org.mx/Medicion/Paginas/Pobreza-y-genero-en-Mexico-2016-2022.aspx","claim":"CONEVAL found that, outside the elderly population, reported monetary-transfer coverage in 2022 was lower than in earlier periods, with women in poverty experiencing a 12.8-percentage-point decline from 2018. Among older adults, coverage exceeded 75%, but the 2018-2022 expansion was larger for men without poverty (+44.8 points) and women without poverty (+33.5) than for men in poverty (+21.3) and women in poverty (+10.6).","locator":"Results summary, paragraphs/bullets at lines 227-233; linked press release dated 6 March 2024","provisional_id":"WEB-5B-024","canonical_fact_key":"mexico_2022_monetary_transfer_coverage_gender_poverty_status","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["5B"],"methodology_ids":["5B.8"],"source_type":"official_gender_disaggregated_poverty_indicator_system","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official CONEVAL indicator system with opened page, explicit gender-poverty disaggregation and a dated supporting release.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Later release specifically reporting the 2016-2022 series and 2022 coverage.","ruler_attribution":"The administration controlled federal transfer-program design, but differential receipt also reflects age structure, eligibility and reporting; evidence supports implementation-level incidence, not personal intent.","contrary_evidence":"Universal old-age benefits substantially expanded coverage for women and men, including those in poverty, even though gains were larger among nonpoor groups.","underlying_fact_key":"mexico_2022_monetary_transfer_coverage_gender_poverty_status","lenses":["5B.8"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON:{"title":"OECD Economic Surveys: Mexico 2022","publisher":"OECD","publication_date":"2022-02","url":"https://www.oecd.org/content/dam/oecd/en/publications/reports/2022/02/oecd-economic-surveys-mexico-2022_a2937d37/2e1de26c-en.pdf","claim":"The OECD contemporaneously recorded that the shift toward nearly universal pensions and scholarships expanded program receipt overall, but the share of households receiving monetary transfers had fallen in the three lowest deciles while rising in the others. It recommended stronger means testing and greater coverage by programs reaching low-income households. Its implementation table also recorded no action on extending preschool hours, while noting 2022 VAT relief for menstrual products, scholarship programs and formalization measures. This supplies dated evidence of selective policy uptake, but not a documented presidential response to the targeting warning itself.","locator":"PDF pp. 54-57, especially paragraphs preceding Figure 1.36, Box 1.10, and Table 1.11; extracted lines 3168-3289","provisional_id":"WEB-5B-025","canonical_fact_key":"oecd_2022_mexico_social_policy_targeting_warning_and_recorded_actions","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["5B"],"methodology_ids":["5B.7","5B.8"],"source_type":"international_organization_policy_review","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous OECD review with household-data incidence, explicit recommendations, and a structured record of actions taken or not taken; it does not establish AMLO's personal deliberations.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Published in 2022 and directly assesses policies and actions in force entering that year.","ruler_attribution":"The administration owned the redesign toward broad nonconditional programs and the cited reforms, but the source does not show a dated AMLO decision accepting or rejecting the OECD targeting recommendation.","contrary_evidence":"The broader model increased total coverage, lowered administrative constraints and expanded support for older people and disabled people; the adverse finding concerns distribution toward the poorest deciles, not absence of benefits.","underlying_fact_key":"oecd_2022_mexico_social_policy_targeting_warning_and_recorded_actions","lenses":["5B.7","5B.8"]}
